Object.assign(T.en,{gb_h:"Government barometer",gb_dem:"Democracy",gb_plur:"Pluralism of those in power",gb_app:"Popularity",gb_trust:"Trust in the government",gb_prom:"Promises kept",gb_cpi:"Integrity (perceived corruption)",gb_reg:["Closed autocracy","Electoral autocracy","Electoral democracy","Liberal democracy"],gb_own:(v,n)=>v==null?"":`Barotico promise-o-meter on ${n}: ${v}/100.`,gb_rank:r=>`rank ${r}`,gb_na:"No comparable figure found yet.",gb_note:"Red is alarming, green is reassuring. Measured by others, never by us, except the promise-o-meter, which uses the sources on each party card.",gb_info:{dem:"V-Dem's Liberal Democracy Index: free and fair elections, freedom of expression and association, independent courts, limits on the executive. 100 = fully democratic. V-Dem also sorts countries into four regimes: closed autocracy (no real elections, e.g. China, North Korea), electoral autocracy (elections without fair competition), electoral democracy and liberal democracy. Rated by thousands of country experts (V-Dem Institute, University of Gothenburg).",plur:"How far the party or parties in power accept the rules of democracy, according to the V-Party expert survey: free elections, respect for opponents and minorities, rejection of political violence. 100 = fully pluralist. It is the same measure as on each party card.",app:"Share of people who approve of, or are satisfied with, the head of the executive in the latest reputable national poll. Green from 55%, yellow 40 to 54%, orange 25 to 39%, red below 25%. In countries V-Dem classes as autocracies the bar stays grey: polls there are too constrained to read as reassuring or alarming.",trust:"Share of people who trust the national government, from a survey that compares countries (OECD Trust Survey, Eurobarometer). Same colour scale as popularity.",prom:"Barotico's promise-o-meter applied to the promises of the party in power: a promise kept with a measured effect counts fully, one partly kept counts half, one kept only on paper a quarter. Independent trackers are mentioned when they exist.",cpi:"Transparency International's Corruption Perceptions Index: how corrupt experts and business people judge the public sector to be. 100 = very clean, 0 = highly corrupt."}});
Object.assign(T.en,{"b_h":"Where public money goes","b_lead":"Twenty years of public spending, debt and growth, with the governments of the time in the background. Spending covers all public administrations (State, local authorities, social security), so countries can be compared.","b_info":"Source: Eurostat, using the international COFOG classification of spending by function, for all public administrations, as a share of GDP. A budget voted one year is often inherited from the previous government, and the big crises (2008, Covid, energy) hit almost every country at once: the chart shows what changed under each government, not who alone caused it. The orientation (left, centre, right) is that of the party leading the executive, as on the party cards.","b_h1":"Spending by function","b_unit":"% of GDP","b_h2":"What rises, what falls","b_h2s":"each curve on its own scale, % of GDP","b_h3":"Debt, deficit and growth","b_mac":{"tot":"Total public spending (% of GDP)","debt":"Public debt (% of GDP)","defc":"Deficit (–) or surplus (% of GDP)","gdp":"Real GDP growth (%)"},"b_cat":{"soc":"Social protection","hea":"Health","edu":"Education","eco":"Economic affairs","int":"Interest on the debt","def":"Defence","sec":"Police, justice, security","oth":"Administration, environment, housing, culture"},"b_ori":{"l":"left","c":"centre","r":"right","n":"no party"},"b_pres":"Background bands: who led the executive.","b_pm":"Prime ministers:","b_src":"Source: Eurostat (October 2026).","b_table":"See the figures","b_year":"Year","b_total":"Total","crisis_fin":"2008 crisis","crisis_covid":"Covid",b_mini_h:"The economy at a glance",b_more:"See spending by function and the governments",b_mob:"Tip: these charts are easier to read on a computer screen.","crisis_energy":"Energy shock"});
